.Ss "2.2.4 Control Expressions"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Li A=>B
If
.Ar A
is not 0, evaluate
.Ar B
(Read as
.Dq Li "A implies B" ) .
Thus,
.Li K=>X_1
does nothing if
.Ar K
is zero, or sets
.Ar X
to 1 if
.Ar K
is non-zero.
.Li X<Y=>(P_1;Q_2\&)
changes
.Ar P
and
.Ar Q
if
.Ar X
is less than
.Ar Y ,
otherwise does nothing.
.Sy "NOTA BENE" :
If
.Ar A
is a relational expression, it need not, and for best object code should
not, be enclosed in parentheses.
If
.Ar A
is a constant, or an expression involving only constants, then the code
generated by the compiler for the expression
.Li A=>B
will consist either of
.Ar B ,
if
.Ar A
is non-zero, or nothing at all, if
.Ar A
is zero.
This allows code to be compiled conditionally if, for example,
.Ar A
is an identifier defined by syntax to be a constant.
In the case that
.Ar B
is not compiled due to
.Ar A
being a constant 0, any tags contained within
.Ar B
will not be defined, but declarations contained in
.Ar B
will take effect.
.It Li A=>B Ic ELSE Ar C
This expression has the value
.Ar B
if
.Ar A
is nonzero, and
.Ar C
if
.Ar A
is zero.
It will evaluate only one of its operands each time it is executed;
thus it can be used to replace the construct
.Dq Li "A=>(B; GO TO FOO); C; FOO\&:"
if the user does not mind the value being computed and ignored (which
costs a few extra instructions of object code).
.Sy "NOTA BENE" :
The expression
.Li "A_B=>X ELSE Y" ,
e.g., will be interpreted as
.Li "(A_B)=>X ELSE Y" .
To assign the obvious interpretation to it, write
.Li "A_(B=>X ELSE Y\&)" .
If
.Ar C
contains another
.Li =>\-ELSE
clause, then
.Ar C
should be enclosed in parentheses.
.It Ar A Ic FOR Ar B Ic IN Li C,D,E
.It Ar A Ic FOR Ar B Ic TO Ar E
.It Ar A Ic FOR Ar B Ic FROM Ar E
These expressions perform repeated execution of the expression A with
different values of the variable
.Ar B .
The
.Ic IN
form executes A with values of
.Ar B
equal to
.Ar C ,
.Li "C+D, C+2*D" , . . . ,
.Ar E ;
the
.Ic TO
form for
.Ar B
equal to
.Li 0,1,...,E ,
and the
.Ic FROM
form for
.Ar B
equal to
.Li E,E\-1,...,0 .
In every case the expression
.Ar A
is evaluated at least once regardless of the values of
.Ar C ,
.Ar D
and
.Ar E .
In the
.Ic IN
case when
.Ar D
is not a constant,
.Ar B
must exactly reach the value of
.Ar E .
E.g., in
.Bd -literal -offset indent
I_2;
(A[J]_0) FOR J IN 0,I,9
.Ed
.Pp
the loop will never terminate.
In all other cases, the loop terminates when
.Ar B
passes the terminal value.
.Ar C ,
.Ar D
and
.Ar E
may be any expressions, but in the
.Ic IN
and
.Ic TO
cases
.Ar D
and
.Ar E
will be evaluated once per iteration of the loop.
The
.Ic FROM
case generates the most efficient code and is therefore to be preferred.
If control is transferred from within the loop, the value of the index
variable
.Ar B
is preserved.
The value of
.Ar B
following normal termination of the loop is undefined.
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
The special cases
.Li "(A[I]__B[I]) FOR I TO/FROM V" ,
where
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
are integer arrays and
.Ar V
is a single variable or constant, are compiled as a block transfer instruction.
The user is cautioned that in the
.Ic FROM
case, if
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
overlap, the code produced will be incorrect.
In more general cases, block transfers may be specified by the
.Ic MOVE
