.Sh "APPENDIX I. UTILITY LIBRARY SUBROUTINES"
The IMP run-time library, file
.Pa IMPLIB.LIB ,
contains a package of subroutines called FORTIO, originally programmed
by Ken Shoemake at the Yale Department of Computer Science to enable
FORTRAN programmers to interface with the DECsystem-10 monitor from
a higher-level language.
These routines are useful to IMP programmers working under DECsystem-10,
and are therefore described here.
.Pp
Many of the functions perform the DECsystem-10 UUO’s of the same name,
and more information about them can be found in the DECsystem-10 Assembly
Language Handbook.
In the listing below, optional arguments are enclosed in angle brackets.
Unless otherwise stated, string arguments to these functions are IMP ASCII
strings.
.Bl -tag -width 10n -compact
.It Fn SAVREG array
saves all registers in the 16-long array array.
.It Fn RSTREG array
restores all registers from the 16-long array array.
.It Fn ARGCNT
returns the number of arguments the calling program was called with;
undefined for a main program.
.It Fn CALLI fn <v1 v2>
performs the
.Li "CALLI UUO" .
Fn is either the function name or the corresponding octal number.
V1, and v2 if appropriate, are the values for
.Ic AC
and
.Li AC+1 .
.Ic CALLI
returns the value returned in the accumulator from the UUO. N.B.: Where
fn is a constant less than 4096 and v1 but not v2 is present, this will
compile as an in-line
.Ic CALLI
(see Section 2.2.9).
.It Fn NOSKIP
returns -1 if the last call to the routine
.Ic CALLI
resulted in a no-skip return, 0 otherwise.
.It Fn SIXBIT stg
returns the ASCII string stg (six characters or less) converted to
.Ic SIXBIT .
.It Fn ASCII sixb
returns the sixbit word sixb, converted to ASCII. The second word of
the result is returned in register
.Li 1R .
.It Fn RAD50 stg <bits>
returns the value of string stg as radix 50, with bits in the four code
bits.
.It Fn RADX50 sixb <bits>
returns the value of the sixbit word sixb as radix 50, with bits in
the four code bits.
.It Fn ASCI50 r50 <bits>
returns the value of the radix 50 symbol r50 converted to ASCII (second
word in register
.Li 1R ) .
If bits is present, it is set to the value of the code bits.
.It Fn SIX50 r50 <bits>
returns the value of the radix 50 symbol r50 converted to sixbit.
If bits is present, it is set to the value of the code bits.
.It Fn OPEN ch ary
performs the
.Li "OPEN UUO"
on channel ch with the address of the three-word array ary as the effective
address.
Returns 0 if no error, -1 otherwise.
.It Fn INIT ch stat <dev obuf ibuf>
performs the
.Ic INIT
UUO with the specified information.
.Ql DSK
is the default device, and if obuf or ibuf contains a left-justified
ASCII
.Ql 0
the corresponding buffer is omitted in the call.
.It Fn INBUF chan n
performs the
.Li "INBUF UUO"
with the specified information.
.It Fn OUTBUF chan n
performs the
.Li "OUTBUF UUO"
with the specified information.
.It Fn RENAME chan ary
.It Fn LOOKUP chan ary
.It Fn ENTER chan ary
.It Fn RELEA chan
releases the specified channel.
.It Fn CLOSE chan <n>
performs the
.Li "CLOSE chan,n"
UUO.
.It Fn IN chan <LOC(arry)>
.It Fn OUT chan <LOC(arry)>
.It Fn GETSTS chan
returns the result of a
.Ic GETSTS
for the specified channel.
.It Fn SETSTS chan status
performs the
.Li "SETSTS UUO"
for the specified channel and status word.
.It Fn STATO chan bits
returns -1 if a
.Ic STATO
for the specified channel gave a skip return, 0 otherwise.
.It Fn STATZ chan bits
returns -1 if a
.Ic STATZ
for the specified channel gave a skip return, 0 otherwise.
.It Fn GETCHA
returns the number of a channel which is not open, starting with channel
.Li 17B .
If no channels are available, returns -1.
.It Fn MTAPE chan n
performs the indicated
.Ic MTAPE
function.
.It Fn UGETF chan
returns the result of a
.Li "UGETF UUO"
on the specified channel.
.It Fn USETI chan blknum
performs the
.Li "USETI UUO"
for the specified channel and block.
.It Fn USETO chan blknum
performs the
.Li "USETO UUO"
for the specified channel and block.
.It Fn INCHRW
returns the value of a right-justified character from the teletype,
waiting until one is typed.
.It Fn OUTCHAR char
types the specified right-justified character on the teletype.
.It Fn INCHRS
returns a character (right-justified) read from the teletype, or -1
if none has been typed.
.It Fn INCHWL
returns a character (right-justified) read from the teletype, waiting
until a break character (e.g., carriage return or altmode) has been
typed.
This is the preferred way to read from the teletype as it allows the
person typing to edit his line using rubout and control-U.
.It Fn INCHSL
same as
.Ic INCHWL ,
but returns -1 if a line has not been typed.
.It Fn RESCAN bit
backs up the teletype scan past one break character, in order to allow
rereading of the last line (possibly a command) typed.
If bit is 1, returns -1 if no command is in the buffer.
.It Fn SKPINC
returns -1 if a character has been typed, 0 otherwise.
.It Fn SKPINL
returns -1 if a line has been typed, 0 otherwise.
.It Fn OUTSTR string
types the specified ASCII string on the teletype.
.It Fn IONEOU char
types a single 8-bit character on the teletype.
.It Fn GETLCH <line>
returns the line characteristics for the specified line; assumes the
caller’s line if none is specified.
.It Fn SETLCH value
sets the line characteristics for the caller’s line.
.It Fn CLRBFI
clears the input buffer.
.It Fn CLRBFO
clears the output buffer.
.El
